backend.mm:1160-1178 (ContextData::BeginCommands):
  if (dev->settingsChanged) { dev->Lock(); ApplyEngineSettingsChange(_owner, dev); dev->Unlock(); }
  SDL_GetWindowSizeInPixels(..., &w, &h); if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return false;
  if (have != wantSize) layer.drawableSize = wantSize;   // the drawable follows the window every frame
  dev->settingsChanged = 0;                                // unconditional, outside the branch and the lock
Shape 1 (minimise): ApplyEngineSettingsChange at line 1120 returns at once when SDL_WINDOW_MINIMIZED is set. A Cocoa window keeps its pixel size when miniaturised, so w and h stay > 0, line 1178 clears the flag, and the request is dropped. Shape 2 (ext.render_pipe): the render thread runs BeginCommands for the pending packet while the main thread pumps SDL. The kExtBeforeSteps fence (mainloop_frame.cpp:363) drains only on frames that step. Video::Device::SizeWindow (device_window.cpp:76, from SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_PIXEL_SIZE_CHANGED), OnWindowEvent (backend.mm:894), ApplyChangedSettings (device.cpp:298) and SunLight::ExtSyncMaxShadowRes (sunlight.cpp:345) write settingsChanged = 1 on the main thread. A write that lands after the read at 1160 and before the clear at 1178 is erased. The flag is a plain uint8_t (Device.h:598) written by both threads with no synchronisation, which is a data race and UB. After a lost resize, SizeWindow has already set backBufferWidth/Height to the new size but mainColor/mainDepth keep the old size. The next BeginCommands sees the flag at 0 and does not rebuild. layer.drawableSize follows the new size, so Present's sameSize test fails on every frame and only a clear is presented (a black window until another resize). The viewport is set from dev->backBufferWidth/Height (lines 1268-1270), so it exceeds the attachment when the window grew (a Metal validation error; undefined without validation). For the sun map, ExtSyncMaxShadowRes has already written _depthTarget->_width = want, so it never raises the flag again (line 331 now compares equal). The descriptor and the real texture then disagree for the whole session. The Vulkan backend (device.cpp:1392-1397) clears the flag only inside the branch that applied it, then refuses the frame.

CreateSwapchain (~493-500):
```
ci.oldSwapchain = swapchain;
r = vkCreateSwapchainKHR(device, &ci, nullptr, &created);
swapchainLost = r == VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST;
if (r != VK_SUCCESS) { *why = ...; return false; }   // `swapchain` still holds the old handle
```
The Vulkan spec (vkCreateSwapchainKHR) says: 'Upon calling vkCreateSwapchainKHR with an oldSwapchain that is not VK_NULL_HANDLE, oldSwapchain is retired -- even if creation of the new swapchain fails.' VUID-VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR-oldSwapchain-01933 requires: 'If oldSwapchain is not VK_NULL_HANDLE, oldSwapchain must be a non-retired swapchain associated with native window referred to by surface'.

Both callers keep the retired handle and retry. ApplyEngineSettingsChange (~592-595) sets `swapchainDirty = true` on failure. BeginCommands (~1413-1425) returns false with swapchainDirty still set, and its comment says the rebuild is 'retried every frame'. The next CreateSwapchain then passes the retired handle as oldSwapchain: a VU violation on every retry. Under the faithful profile (fix.boot_device_lost off), a vkCreateSwapchainKHR that answered VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST is retried this way on every frame for good.

